W: 1. As I roved out on a bright May morning
W: To view the meadows and the flowers gay
W: Whom should I spy but my own true lover
W: As she sat under yon willow tree.
W:
W: 2. I took off my hat and I did salute her
W: I did salute her most courageously
W: When she turned around well the tears fell from her
W: Saying, “False young man, you’ve deluded me.”
W:
W: 3. A diamond ring I owned I gave you
W: A diamond ring to wear on your right hand
W: But the vows you made, love, you went and broke them
W: And married the lassie that had the land.
W:
W: 4. If I’d married the lassie that had the land, my love
W: It’s that I’ll rue till the day I die.
W: When misfortune falls sure no man can shun it
W: I was blindfolded I’ll ne’er deny.
W:
W: 5. Now at nights when I go to my bed of slumber
W: My thoughts of my true love run in my mind
W: When I turned around to embrace my darling
W: Instead of gold sure it’s brass I find.
W:
W: 6. And I wish the Queen would call home her army
W: From the West Indies, America and Spain
W: And every man to his wedded woman
W: In hopes that you and I will meet again.
